\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related-work}

\looseness=-1
\paragraph{Self-consistency and ensemble theory.}
Self-consistency (SC) selects the most frequent answer across chain-of-thought traces \citep{wang2023sc}. Extensions address free-form generation \citep{chen2024usc}, confidence weighting \citep{taubenfeld2025cisc}, diverse prompting \citep{li2023diverse}, and reasoning-aware filtering \citep{wan2024rasc, toh2024notallvotes, kim2026reasc}. SC gains plateau early \citep{loo2025reevaluating, byerly2026selfconsistency}. The Condorcet Jury Theorem \citep{condorcet1785} guarantees majority-vote convergence under independence; \citet{ladha1992correlated} show correlated voters reduce effective ensemble size. Our $K_\text{eff}$ adapts Fleiss' $\kappa$ from ensemble diversity theory \citep{kuncheva2003measures, brown2005diversity} to quantify this compression (\S\ref{sec:pipeline}). \citet{kim2025correlated} demonstrate correlated errors in LLM outputs; \citet{kuai2026independent} propose cross-model independence auditing, complementary to our within-model process-$\kappa$.

\looseness=-1
\paragraph{Neuro-symbolic reasoning.}
We distinguish three paradigms: \emph{specification-generation} \citep{pan2023logiclm, olausson2023linc, ye2024satlm} translates problems into formal specifications before answering; \emph{program-generation} \citep{gao2023pal, chen2023program, lyu2023faithful} produces executable code; both formalize \emph{before} answer generation. \emph{Trace-extraction} (this work) operates post-hoc, inheriting confirmation bias (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}). Recent extensions expand these paradigms with symbolic planning, logic agents, and verified reasoning \citep{ryu2025clover, xu2026dylos, li2025aristotle, wang2025fover, singh2026verge, luo2025logicagent, miya2025eidoku}; \citet{amonkar2025realitycheck} show formalization quality degrades with problem complexity; \citet{chan2026position} argue logical soundness alone is unreliable for neurosymbolic verification. Prior studies \citep{olausson2023linc, amonkar2025realitycheck} note high formalization error rates but treat them as quality problems; we show the errors are \emph{systematically biased} toward the model's answer. Proposition~1 establishes that self-extracted constraints reduce SICA to majority vote under degeneration ($C \perp Y \mid A$; Figure~\ref{fig:causal-dag}), making self-verification an information bottleneck. \citet{huang2024selfcorrect} show LLMs cannot self-correct without external feedback; our findings provide independent evidence consistent with this limitation in formal verification.

\looseness=-1
\paragraph{Distinction from CoT confirmation bias.}
Symbolic confirmation bias (SCB) is complementary to, but structurally distinct from, CoT-level confirmation bias \citep{wan2025unveiling, turpin2024language, jhaveri2026failing}. CoT bias operates at the \emph{trace level}---individual chains may not faithfully reflect the model's computation---while SCB operates at the \emph{aggregation level}: even if each trace is locally faithful, the \emph{collection} of self-extracted constraints mirrors the answer distribution. Methodologically, SCB is formalized via a Markov chain structure (Proposition~1, Remark~1) and confirmed through systematic exclusion of 27 training-free alternatives (\S\ref{sec:remediation}). The key distinction: CoT bias implies individual traces may be unfaithful; SCB shows that even Z3-validated constraints carry no information beyond majority voting when self-extracted.

\looseness=-1
\paragraph{Test-time compute and self-verification.}
Scaling test-time compute via additional traces \citep{snell2025scaling}, structured search \citep{yao2023tree}, or extended reasoning \citep{guo2025deepseekr1, qwen2024qwq} faces the same correlation challenge when traces share a single model's biases. Multi-agent debate \citep{liang2023encouraging, du2023improving}, self-verification and refinement \citep{weng2023selfverification, madaan2023selfrefine, dhuliawala2023chainofverification}, iterative self-improvement \citep{shinn2023reflexion, zhou2023lats}, tool-augmented critique \citep{gou2024critic}, formal CoT verification \citep{zhu2025vericot, chen2025sets}, and multi-agent verification \citep{lifshitz2025mav} report gains, but none tests self-extracted constraints under formal solvers \citep[cf.][]{kamoi2024selfcorrect}. We find the bottleneck lies in constraint extraction, not verification: self-extracted constraints inherit the model's bias regardless of solver soundness (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}). Oracle controls confirm the verification mechanism itself is sound (Appendix~\ref{app:oracle}).

\looseness=-1
\paragraph{Training-based verification.}
The methods above operate at inference time without additional training. Trained verifiers can escape the self-extraction bottleneck via external reward signals: outcome and process reward models \citep{cobbe2021gsm8k, lightman2024prm, wang2024mathshepherd}, RL-based self-verification \citep{zeng2025s2r}, dual preference optimization \citep{dupo2025}, and execution-based filtering in code \citep{li2022alphacode, chen2023codet}. Proposition~1 addresses inference-time self-extraction; trained verifiers operate outside this pipeline, potentially breaking the information ceiling (Remark~1) by violating the trace-determined assumption.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{SICA Pipeline}
\label{sec:pipeline}
\label{sec:condorcet}
\label{sec:effective-k}

Self-Consistency (SC) \citep{wang2023sc} samples $K$ reasoning traces and selects the majority answer. Under correlated traces (Fleiss' $\kappa$ \citep{fleiss1971kappa}), effective sample size reduces to
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:effk}
K_{\text{eff}} = K \,/\, [1 + (K{-}1) \cdot \kappa]
\end{equation}
(following \citet{ladha1992correlated}); e.g., $K{=}12$ traces with $\kappa{=}0.69$ yield $K_\text{eff} \approx 1.3$, meaning twelve correlated traces carry no more independent information than 1.3 uncorrelated ones. This motivates constraint-based aggregation.

Symbolic Invariant Consensus Aggregation (SICA) extends SC by extracting formal constraints from traces and selecting the answer best supported by the satisfiable subset (Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline}).

\paragraph{Trace generation.} Given question $q$, an LLM generates $K$ traces $\{t_1, \ldots, t_K\}$ at temperature $T = 0.7$. Each trace $t_i$ yields answer $a_i$.

\looseness=-1
\paragraph{Constraint extraction.}\label{sec:constraint-extraction} The same LLM extracts logical constraints from each trace at $T_{\text{ext}} = 0.3$. The extraction prompt requests JSON output with constraint type, expression, and Z3-compatible formula (Appendix~\ref{app:prompts}); in practice, 99.0\% of extracted constraints are propositional at the Z3 level, with only 1.0\% using true quantifiers. The \emph{extraction rate} is the fraction of traces yielding at least one parseable constraint.

\paragraph{Deduplication and MAX-SAT.} Constraints are deduplicated by syntactic equivalence (global average deduplication ratio: 46.0\%); each unique constraint $c_m$ receives weight $w_m$ equal to the number of source traces. The objective finds the largest-weight satisfiable subset:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:maxsat}
\mathcal{C}^* = \arg\max_{\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{C}} \sum_{c \in \mathcal{S}} w_c \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \mathcal{S} \text{ is satisfiable.}
\end{equation}
Z3 \citep{demoura2008z3} solves this with a 10-second timeout. Answer assertion constraints ($\texttt{answer\_a} == \texttt{True}$, weight 1) ensure every candidate receives a nonzero base score, making SICA a strict generalization of SC.

\paragraph{Scoring.} Let $\mathcal{C}^{\setminus} = \mathcal{C} \setminus \mathcal{C}^*$. Each candidate $a$ is scored by:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:score}
\text{score}(a) = \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}^*} w_c \cdot \sigma(c, a) - \alpha \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}^{\setminus}} w_c \cdot \sigma(c, a),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(c, a) = 1$ if $c$ was extracted from any trace producing answer $a$, and $\alpha = 0.5$. The selected answer is $a^* = \arg\max_{a} \text{score}(a)$.

\paragraph{Degeneration condition.} If self-extracted constraints are coupled to their source answer, MAX-SAT retains nearly all ($|\mathcal{C}^{\setminus}| \approx 0$), and scoring degenerates to:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:degenerate}
\text{score}(a) \approx \sum_{\substack{c \in \mathcal{C}^* \\ \text{source}(c) \to a}} w_c,
\end{equation}
equivalent to majority voting. We show in \S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias} that this degeneration occurs in practice.

\paragraph{The $C_0$ diagnostic.} The multi-candidate rate $C_0 = |\{q : |\mathcal{A}(q)| > 1\}| / |\mathcal{Q}|$ is a zero-cost diagnostic: $C_0 < 5\%$ means SICA reduces to SC. $\alpha = 0.5$ is not critical (${\pm}0.49$\,pp across $\alpha \in [0, 1]$).

\paragraph{Bias ratio.} We quantify extraction bias via the \emph{bias ratio}:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mirage}
\text{BR}(M,T) = \frac{P(c \text{ supports } a_M \mid a_M \neq a^*)}{P(c \text{ supports } a^* \mid a_M \neq a^*)},
\end{equation}
where $c$ is a self-extracted constraint, $a_M$ the model's majority answer, and $a^*$ the ground truth. BR quantifies the degree to which extracted constraints align with the majority answer; values above 1 are expected under source-attribution scoring (Proposition~1) and reflect both the base-rate effect of trace-count distributions and any additional extraction alignment. $\text{BR} = 1$ indicates unbiased extraction. We validate this metric empirically in \S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}.

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:exp-setup}

\looseness=-1
\paragraph{Models.} Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, Qwen3-14B (non-thinking mode)~\citep{yang2024qwen25}, Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3~\citep{jiang2023mistral}, LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct~\citep{dubey2024llama3}, and QwQ-32B~\citep{qwen2024qwq} (7B--32B). We also evaluate the universality of symbolic confirmation bias via cross-model extraction on five frontier models (gpt-4o, gpt-4.1, gpt-5.5, o3, Gemini-2.5-Pro; Table~\ref{tab:frontier-scb}); Mistral-7B extracts constraints from target traces since closed-source APIs do not support self-extraction.

\paragraph{Datasets.} FOLIO~\citep{han2022folio} ($n{=}204$, with gold logical annotations), ProofWriter depth-5~\citep{tafjord2021proofwriter} ($n{=}600$), LogiQA~\citep{liu2020logiqa} ($n{=}200$). StrategyQA~\citep{geva2021strategyqa} ($n{=}200$) serves as a domain-formalizability diagnostic. GSM8K~\citep{cobbe2021gsm8k} (exploratory, $n{=}30$) and AIME 2024 ($n{=}22$) probe the degenerate regime ($C_0 < 5\%$).

\paragraph{Parameters.} $K{=}12$ traces at $T{=}0.7$, extraction at $T_{\text{ext}}{=}0.3$, $\alpha{=}0.5$, Z3 timeout 10s. McNemar exact test ($\alpha{=}0.05$); 95\% bootstrap CIs ($10{,}000$ resamples) for $n \geq 200$. SICA incurs $4.5{\times}$ SC wall-clock; extraction accounts for 76\% of overhead.

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

\looseness=-1
Self-Consistency (SC) \citep{wang2023sc} selects the majority answer across $K$ chain-of-thought traces, a strategy grounded in the Condorcet Jury Theorem: independent voters converge to truth \citep{condorcet1785, ladha1992correlated}. In practice, SC's gains plateau for strong models \citep{loo2025reevaluating}, pointing to correlated traces that reduce the effective ensemble size. A natural remedy is to move beyond simple vote-counting: extract logical constraints from the traces and let a provably sound solver adjudicate. If the solver can filter contradictions and enforce logical consistency, it should recover information that raw majority voting discards.

\looseness=-1
We test this intuition through Symbolic Invariant Consensus Aggregation (SICA), which extracts logical constraints from reasoning traces (prompted as first-order logic; 99\% are propositional at the Z3 level) and selects answers via weighted partial MAX-SAT (\S\ref{sec:pipeline}; Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline}). The result is unequivocal: despite Z3 validating nearly all self-extracted constraints, SICA produces answers identical to majority voting on the vast majority of problems. Across six models (7B--32B), three reasoning domains, and multi-seed replication, no condition shows a significant positive effect after FDR control (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}). Four conditions confirm equivalence within $\pm$2\,pp; ten remain underpowered for effects below ${\sim}$2\,pp.

\looseness=-1
The failure is not in the solver but in the constraints it operates on. Self-extracted constraints systematically support the model's majority answer, a phenomenon we term \emph{symbolic confirmation bias} (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}). Because each trace determines both an answer and its constraints, the formal channel reduces to a weighted echo of the majority vote. We prove this degeneration is structural (Observation~1) and show it holds across nine models from 7B to frontier scale. Prior work shows LLMs cannot self-correct reasoning without external feedback \citep{huang2024selfcorrect}; we provide independent evidence that this limitation extends to formal self-verification.

\looseness=-1
Twenty-seven training-free variants within the constraint-extraction paradigm confirm the trade-off is fundamental (\S\ref{sec:remediation}): methods that break trace correlation destroy accuracy, while methods that preserve accuracy fail to achieve independence. Cross-architecture verification provides preliminary evidence of escape: a structurally independent verifier yields significant gains when it exceeds the generator's accuracy, with benefit governed by a capability-gap pattern between verifier and generator (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}). Three zero-cost diagnostics enable practitioners to predict a priori whether any self-extraction-based aggregation method will help (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic-framework}).

Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{The symbolic mirage.} Provably sound Z3 verification becomes vacuous under self-extraction. We systematically diagnose symbolic confirmation bias as the mechanism, identify the structural degeneration (Observation~1), and confirm it empirically across nine models and 27 training-free variants, localizing the failure to extraction rather than verification (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}, \S\ref{sec:remediation}).

\item \textbf{Diagnostic framework.} Three zero-cost diagnostics characterize failure modes across all tested conditions within the self-extraction paradigm, with prospective validation on held-out model families, providing practitioners with a priori failure-mode diagnosis from a pilot run (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic-framework}).

\item \textbf{Cross-architecture escape.} Preliminary evidence that structurally independent verification can break the bottleneck, with gain governed by a capability-gap pattern between verifier and generator (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}).
\end{enumerate}

